WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.041
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4548.

2
00:00:01.041 --> 00:00:02.082
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.082 --> 00:00:02.776
<v REBECCA COOPER>Okay, great.

4
00:00:02.776 --> 00:00:04.857
So today is November twenty-third, 2015.

5
00:00:04.857 --> 00:00:11.102
I'm Becca Cooper representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

6
00:00:11.102 --> 00:00:16.653
We're working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville [Louisiana].

7
00:00:16.653 --> 00:00:22.204
So I'm here today with Mrs. Evelyn Gasaway Shelton at her home in Lake Charles [Louisiana].

8
00:00:22.204 --> 00:00:24.633
This is her second time being interviewed.

9
00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:28.102
So yeah, thank you for agreeing to be interviewed again.

10
00:00:28.102 --> 00:00:28.449
<v EVELYN GASAWAY SHELTON>Sure.

11
00:00:28.449 --> 00:00:33.653
<v COOPER>Last time you shared a bit of background information on your parents and your grandparents,

12
00:00:33.653 --> 00:00:38.510
and you gave a brief overview of education and entertainment growing up in Mossville.

13
00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:44.408
So today we're going to delve a little bit deeper into your experience growing up in Mossville.

14
00:00:44.408 --> 00:00:49.265
To start, you mentioned a bit about your mother's parents, Della and David [Spikes?].

15
00:00:49.265 --> 00:00:50.306
Is that right?

16
00:00:50.306 --> 00:00:51.000
<v SHELTON>[Agrees]

17
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.188
<v COOPER>So what do you remember about them?

18
00:00:55.188 --> 00:01:02.964
<v SHELTON>They were some of the greatest grandparents you'd ever want to come across.

19
00:01:02.964 --> 00:01:04.759
I remember that.

20
00:01:04.759 --> 00:01:07.152
My grandfather was short.

21
00:01:07.152 --> 00:01:11.938
My grandmother was a little taller than him.

22
00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:18.518
As a matter of fact, my grandmother looked somewhat like you,

23
00:01:18.518 --> 00:01:28.688
except that she might have been a bit taller. [laughs] She was a very pretty, pretty lady.

24
00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:32.875
Both of them were very compassionate persons.

25
00:01:32.875 --> 00:01:40.652
My grandfather and grandmother lived in a town that was called Bancroft [Louisiana].

26
00:01:40.652 --> 00:01:44.839
My grandfather farmed and he grew corn.

27
00:01:44.839 --> 00:01:50.223
I don't know . . . Oh, just a lot of vegetables.

28
00:01:50.223 --> 00:02:05.777
What I remember that really stood out was when we would go there on summer vacations and he would get us out of the bed early.

29
00:02:05.777 --> 00:02:11.161
He wanted . . . He had us go into his field.

30
00:02:11.161 --> 00:02:22.527
We hadn't been in the field before, but he had us to go in and try to pick cotton.

31
00:02:22.527 --> 00:02:27.312
Oh, we thought that was a big deal.

32
00:02:27.312 --> 00:02:39.277
So we did, and my sister and I, we'd try to outdo each other as to filling up the sacks.

33
00:02:39.277 --> 00:02:48.848
Well everything that we saw on the bush, we picked that green bulbs and cotton, everything.

34
00:02:48.848 --> 00:03:00.214
So he looked at our bags and he saw where we had . . . we were stripping the plants just about,

35
00:03:00.214 --> 00:03:03.205
so he told us "Nuh-uh.

36
00:03:03.205 --> 00:03:05.000
Never again."

37
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.500
He didn't call us children; he called us "chaps."

38
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:18.500
Later on as I grew older I found out that that was a German word for children, "chaps."

39
00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:30.500
"Oh your chaps are going inside, and don't you ever come out here anymore." [laughs] We thought we were really doing a big deal.

40
00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:37.000
That was our last time of ever going into any fields or anything.

41
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:41.500
He had plum trees . . . well fruit trees, I'd say.

42
00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:48.500
You couldn't just go out there and pick fruits . . . plums or peaches or anything.

43
00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:57.500
He would have to do that for us, not us, because you couldn't go up in the trees.

44
00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:04.000
I don't know why they were so particular about all that, you know?

45
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:11.000
But he was a character when he'd come see his plants and whatnot.

46
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.380
We'd go to church on Sundays.

47
00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:26.211
It looked like we stay in church all day just about then. [laughs] Well they didn't have anything else to do.

48
00:04:26.211 --> 00:04:32.972
The ladies would bring covered dishes and we'd stay there and eat.

49
00:04:32.972 --> 00:04:36.915
Go back in the church later on.

50
00:04:36.915 --> 00:04:43.113
Those are some of the things that I remember really well.

51
00:04:43.113 --> 00:04:45.930
Oh, we'd have family reunions.

52
00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:51.000
They would be out at Grandpa's and Grandmother's house.

53
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.817
They had a log cabin.

54
00:04:53.817 --> 00:04:59.451
They would cook the sweet potatoes in the fire place.

55
00:04:59.451 --> 00:05:12.408
They'd push the . . . I guess the ashes back and put the potatoes in there and cover the potatoes up with the ashes again.

56
00:05:12.408 --> 00:05:15.789
They also did syrup, making syrup.

57
00:05:15.789 --> 00:05:29.873
We thought that was really neat how they would take the horse and they'd go round, and around, and around in circles making the syrup.

58
00:05:29.873 --> 00:05:33.817
But those were the good ole days.

59
00:05:33.817 --> 00:05:42.268
Oh, and my grandfather would not eat out of nothing but a solid white plates.

60
00:05:42.268 --> 00:05:45.085
Nothing with designs or anything.

61
00:05:45.085 --> 00:05:48.465
It had to be solid white.

62
00:05:48.465 --> 00:05:56.915
I couldn’t understand how he lived so long, because when my grandmother would fix grits,

63
00:05:56.915 --> 00:06:03.113
well he'd have a tendency to . . . well she cooked his bacon,

64
00:06:03.113 --> 00:06:10.437
but he'd use the drippings off the bacon and put on his grits.

65
00:06:10.437 --> 00:06:18.324
As I've gotten older, I said, "Well gosh, how did Grandfather live so long?

66
00:06:18.324 --> 00:06:34.099
Ninety-something years old and he'd take that drips to put on his grits." [laughs] But the things that they did back there . . . But everything was natural, I guess.

67
00:06:34.099 --> 00:06:40.296
No preservatives and all that kind of stuff in the food.

68
00:06:40.296 --> 00:06:47.056
But those were some of my memories of my grandmother and grandfather.

69
00:06:47.056 --> 00:06:51.000
<v COOPER>So that was in Bancroft, Louisiana?

70
00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:51.718
<v SHELTON>Bancroft, Louisiana.

71
00:06:51.718 --> 00:06:57.821
<v COOPER>And how did they get to . . . I think you mentioned that your grandfather had come from Georgia.

72
00:06:57.821 --> 00:06:59.974
<v SHELTON>Yeah, that's what he told us.

73
00:06:59.974 --> 00:07:01.769
He was a freed slave.

74
00:07:01.769 --> 00:07:05.000
<v COOPER>And how did they end up in Louisiana?

75
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:06.629
<v SHELTON>I don't know.

76
00:07:06.629 --> 00:07:09.886
I never did find that out.

77
00:07:09.886 --> 00:07:22.914
I'm sure some of my uncles could have told, but at that time we weren't inquisitive as to how they got here or anything.

78
00:07:22.914 --> 00:07:31.600
Not until they had passed on, and then we wondered how they got here to Louisiana.

79
00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.857
But he was a freed slave.

80
00:07:34.857 --> 00:07:37.571
His original name was Culpepper.

81
00:07:37.571 --> 00:07:39.200
I remember that.

82
00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:39.743
Culpepper.

83
00:07:39.743 --> 00:07:48.971
But after he got to Louisiana, I guess his slave owner gave him his [last] name, [Spikes?].

84
00:07:48.971 --> 00:07:49.514
<v COOPER>Wow.

85
00:07:49.514 --> 00:07:58.200
And then . . . Okay, so in your last interview you also mentioned your parents came to Mossville . . .

86
00:07:58.200 --> 00:07:59.286
<v SHELTON>My daddy.

87
00:07:59.286 --> 00:08:00.914
<v COOPER>. . . for the industry.

88
00:08:00.914 --> 00:08:02.000
Right?

89
00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:03.721
<v SHELTON>Yeah, back in 1943.

90
00:08:03.721 --> 00:08:13.616
A lot of people were coming this way in 19 . . . back there in the early '40s, and he came here in the 1943.

91
00:08:13.616 --> 00:08:15.337
He came in 1943.

92
00:08:15.337 --> 00:08:20.070
<v COOPER>Were they brought in by a specific petrochemical or industrial plant?

93
00:08:20.070 --> 00:08:24.372
<v SHELTON>Yeah, Cities Service [Gasaway adds: He came on his own.

94
00:08:24.372 --> 00:08:26.953
He’d heard about the industries here].

95
00:08:26.953 --> 00:08:27.814
<v COOPER>Cities Service.

96
00:08:27.814 --> 00:08:32.547
<v SHELTON>[Agrees] He came here and got a job with Cities Service.

97
00:08:32.547 --> 00:08:35.558
That's where he worked until he retired.

98
00:08:35.558 --> 00:08:39.000
<v COOPER>What did he do at Cities Service?

99
00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:44.247
<v SHELTON>Well, he started off by, I guess . . . oh, common labor[er].

100
00:08:44.247 --> 00:08:47.395
He started off with common labor.

101
00:08:47.395 --> 00:08:51.593
Eventually he ended up being a truck driver.

102
00:08:51.593 --> 00:08:53.167
He drove trucks.

103
00:08:53.167 --> 00:08:59.463
He was a fork lifter for a while and a truck driver.

104
00:08:59.463 --> 00:09:03.136
That was . . . He did that for years.

105
00:09:03.136 --> 00:09:03.660
Yeah.

106
00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:15.204
As a matter fact, as a result of his working there, I was able to get their scholarship to go to college.

107
00:09:15.204 --> 00:09:15.728
<v COOPER>Wow.

108
00:09:15.728 --> 00:09:17.827
<v SHELTON>That's where he was.

109
00:09:17.827 --> 00:09:20.451
He relocated here in '43.

110
00:09:20.451 --> 00:09:31.469
<v COOPER>And so he went to . . . he went up to the eighth grade, and you mentioned your mom went through to college.

111
00:09:31.469 --> 00:09:34.093
<v SHELTON>My mother finished high school . . .

112
00:09:34.093 --> 00:09:34.617
<v COOPER>Yeah.

113
00:09:34.617 --> 00:09:44.062
<v SHELTON>. . . and spent a couple semesters in college until she met my dad and she didn't go back then.

114
00:09:44.062 --> 00:09:48.784
But she insisted her children would go to school.

115
00:09:48.784 --> 00:09:57.179
Well both of them did because she didn't get a chance to go back and finish.

116
00:09:57.179 --> 00:10:04.000
That was always one of her dreams, to go back and finish.

117
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:10.350
<v COOPER>And where did she go to school before she met your father?

118
00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:11.938
<v SHELTON>Weirgate High School.

119
00:10:11.938 --> 00:10:29.401
And you know, back then they had kings and queens for the school because she said she was named Ms. Weirgate at one time. [laughs] So they had kings and queens back then.

120
00:10:29.401 --> 00:10:30.989
We were shocked.

121
00:10:30.989 --> 00:10:36.281
<v COOPER>Okay, so you mentioned family reunions at your grandpa's house.

122
00:10:36.281 --> 00:10:38.398
What were those like?

123
00:10:38.398 --> 00:10:39.456
<v SHELTON>Oh, fun!

124
00:10:39.456 --> 00:10:50.569
My uncles . . . We were always . . . I guess petted because all . . . it seemed like everybody was always much older than we were.

125
00:10:50.569 --> 00:11:02.741
I can remember them, my uncles, getting out there in the yard playing, we called them, ukuleles, the guitar, and singing and dancing.

126
00:11:02.741 --> 00:11:14.912
They'd have a big fire out there in the front yard, because the house had a big porch across the front of it.

127
00:11:14.912 --> 00:11:20.204
My grandparent's house had a big porch across the front.

128
00:11:20.204 --> 00:11:27.613
There was . . . they called them lanes that you drive up the lane, they said.

129
00:11:27.613 --> 00:11:32.376
We could hear certain cars come from a distance.

130
00:11:32.376 --> 00:11:45.077
"There comes so-and-so," because we knew that particular car made a certain noise and we knew they were coming in at that particular time.

131
00:11:45.077 --> 00:11:48.781
But I know I'm kind of rambling.

132
00:11:48.781 --> 00:11:54.602
But those were good times, getting out there singing and dancing.

133
00:11:54.602 --> 00:12:04.128
I could see my uncles getting my grandmother up off her seat to make her dance with them.

134
00:12:04.128 --> 00:12:14.712
It's just some of these . . . You see on some of these cowboy movies where you see them just dancing around.

135
00:12:14.712 --> 00:12:19.474
Not all this hip-hop stuff that they're doing now.

136
00:12:19.474 --> 00:12:29.000
It was just a lot of . . . just jumping around [to my idea?], but they called it dancing.

137
00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:33.915
<v COOPER>And how would you get to Bancroft for the family reunions?

138
00:12:33.915 --> 00:12:36.149
<v SHELTON>How did we get there?

139
00:12:36.149 --> 00:12:36.596
<v COOPER>[Agrees]

140
00:12:36.596 --> 00:12:40.170
<v SHELTON>My dad would take us in his car.

141
00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:43.298
<v COOPER>What kind of car did he have?

142
00:12:43.298 --> 00:12:44.191
<v SHELTON>A Plymouth.

143
00:12:44.191 --> 00:12:48.660
I don't know . . . later on he got him a Buick.

144
00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:50.894
Later on, old Cadillac [laughs].

145
00:12:50.894 --> 00:12:58.043
But he was really one of the few who had automobiles back there at that time.

146
00:12:58.043 --> 00:13:01.170
One of the few, like I said.

147
00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:08.766
I remember when he would . . . some of the ladies in our immediate neighborhood, right around my house,

148
00:13:08.766 --> 00:13:18.596
would have a baby, then he would take them to the hospital and you could hear his car over on Highway 90.

149
00:13:18.596 --> 00:13:20.830
We lived on Prater Road.

150
00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:28.872
You could hear his car on Highway 90 going to the hospital because it made that much noise.

151
00:13:28.872 --> 00:13:31.106
But it would run though.

152
00:13:31.106 --> 00:13:32.000
<v COOPER>Wow.

153
00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:37.806
<v SHELTON>But see, we didn't have but just the two of . . . two girls at that time.

154
00:13:37.806 --> 00:13:40.903
But later on my mother had other kids.

155
00:13:40.903 --> 00:13:44.000
<v COOPER>So how many siblings did you have?

156
00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.538
<v SHELTON>I had five.

157
00:13:45.538 --> 00:13:47.588
Yeah, I had five.

158
00:13:47.588 --> 00:13:49.639
<v COOPER>What were their names?

159
00:13:49.639 --> 00:13:53.227
<v SHELTON>I had one . . . You said their names?

160
00:13:53.227 --> 00:13:53.739
<v COOPER>Yeah.

161
00:13:53.739 --> 00:13:55.790
What are their names?

162
00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:56.303
<v SHELTON>Okay.

163
00:13:56.303 --> 00:14:00.403
The sister next to me was named Lilly.

164
00:14:00.403 --> 00:14:03.992
She was named after my daddy's mother.

165
00:14:03.992 --> 00:14:11.681
My baby sister, who was next to Lilly . . . There were three girls and three boys.

166
00:14:11.681 --> 00:14:16.294
The girls were born first and then the boys.

167
00:14:16.294 --> 00:14:18.857
The next one was Della.

168
00:14:18.857 --> 00:14:23.471
She was named after my grandmother, my mother's mother.

169
00:14:23.471 --> 00:14:29.109
Let me see . . . And then we had one boy to die.

170
00:14:29.109 --> 00:14:32.185
He was named after my father.

171
00:14:32.185 --> 00:14:34.235
He was Casey Junior.

172
00:14:34.235 --> 00:14:35.773
Next was Larry.

173
00:14:35.773 --> 00:14:38.336
Then Jimmy, and then Billy.

174
00:14:38.336 --> 00:14:41.412
Those were . . . that was the family.

175
00:14:41.412 --> 00:14:45.000
<v COOPER>Where did you fit in age-wise?

176
00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:46.023
<v SHELTON>I was oldest.

177
00:14:46.023 --> 00:14:46.705
<v COOPER>The oldest.

178
00:14:46.705 --> 00:14:47.045
<v SHELTON>Yeah.

179
00:14:47.045 --> 00:14:50.114
And they say I was the most spoiled one.

180
00:14:50.114 --> 00:14:53.523
I don't think so, but that's what they said [laughs].

181
00:14:53.523 --> 00:14:56.591
<v COOPER>What were your chores growing up in the family?

182
00:14:56.591 --> 00:15:00.000
Or did you have certain responsibilities as the oldest?

183
00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:00.496
<v SHELTON>Yeah.

184
00:15:00.496 --> 00:15:05.452
We had to keep our rooms such as we had.

185
00:15:05.452 --> 00:15:07.435
Keep our room neat.

186
00:15:07.435 --> 00:15:10.904
Fold clothes when my mother would wash.

187
00:15:10.904 --> 00:15:14.870
Hang clothes on the . . . out on the line.

188
00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:17.348
They had clothes lines then.

189
00:15:17.348 --> 00:15:23.296
So we had to hang our clothes out on the clothes lines.

190
00:15:23.296 --> 00:15:30.235
No cooking because you couldn't get in that kitchen and mess up food [laughs].

191
00:15:30.235 --> 00:15:34.200
So we didn't do any cooking and such.

192
00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:40.148
We could watch my mother cook, but we couldn't do any cooking.

193
00:15:40.148 --> 00:15:46.591
We could wash the dishes, now, but not do any of the cooking.

194
00:15:46.591 --> 00:15:54.522
<v COOPER>And what was your house like that you grew up in, the one on Prater road?

195
00:15:54.522 --> 00:15:57.000
Can you describe it?

196
00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:57.573
<v SHELTON>Okay.

197
00:15:57.573 --> 00:16:06.170
The first house that I grew up in, that was up until 1950- something . . . 1950.

198
00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:14.767
I don't remember the one we lived in when my parents first moved to Mossville.

199
00:16:14.767 --> 00:16:19.925
I know that it was near the railroad tracks.

200
00:16:19.925 --> 00:16:29.095
It was like North West of the railroad track, before you get to the recreation center.

201
00:16:29.095 --> 00:16:35.399
But I think . . . I know that house was . . . no I don't.

202
00:16:35.399 --> 00:16:38.838
I don't remember that such [house?].

203
00:16:38.838 --> 00:16:43.423
All I knew was where it was located.

204
00:16:43.423 --> 00:16:52.593
The next one that we lived in, I remember that one, which was on Prater Road.

205
00:16:52.593 --> 00:16:58.897
It was about four bed . . . I mean four rooms, not bedrooms.

206
00:16:58.897 --> 00:17:00.043
Four rooms.

207
00:17:00.043 --> 00:17:09.787
They had a room for my parents, room for the girls, a kitchen and then another room.

208
00:17:09.787 --> 00:17:13.225
Now we didn't have any electricity.

209
00:17:13.225 --> 00:17:26.407
That's what I can recall, not having any electricity, because I remember having to study by the lights from the lamps, kerosene lamps.

210
00:17:26.407 --> 00:17:32.711
We kept warm from the heated stove, wood stove and whatnot.

211
00:17:32.711 --> 00:17:42.455
I remember alongside of the stove there was always a water thing that they kept water in.

212
00:17:42.455 --> 00:17:46.466
I don't know what that was for.

213
00:17:46.466 --> 00:17:51.051
A water reservoir was what it was called.

214
00:17:51.051 --> 00:18:01.368
But that was always on the side of this big stove that they had in this little house.

215
00:18:01.368 --> 00:18:09.391
That was the extent . . . That was the way the house looked at that time.

216
00:18:09.391 --> 00:18:10.538
Four rooms.

217
00:18:10.538 --> 00:18:15.696
<v COOPER>And that was the one on Old Spanish Trail?

218
00:18:15.696 --> 00:18:17.988
Or on Prater Road?

219
00:18:17.988 --> 00:18:19.708
<v SHELTON>On Prater Road.

220
00:18:19.708 --> 00:18:22.000
<v COOPER>On Prater Road.

221
00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:32.655
<v SHELTON>The one on Old Spanish Trail I can't . . . All I know, it was north west of the railroad tracks.

222
00:18:32.655 --> 00:18:38.264
It was long, but that's all I remember of it.

223
00:18:38.264 --> 00:18:43.872
That was very small when we came as a toddler.

224
00:18:43.872 --> 00:18:50.041
<v COOPER>Did you have a garden at the house at Prater Road?

225
00:18:50.041 --> 00:18:53.966
Or did your mom have a garden?

226
00:18:53.966 --> 00:18:56.770
<v SHELTON>Did we have a garden?

227
00:18:56.770 --> 00:18:59.574
Not until my parents built.

228
00:18:59.574 --> 00:19:04.622
I can't recall them having a garden before then.

229
00:19:04.622 --> 00:19:14.155
My daddy bought some acreage on the other side of this little four bedroom . . . four room house.

230
00:19:14.155 --> 00:19:19.764
He bought acreage on the south side of that house.

231
00:19:19.764 --> 00:19:32.662
After they built in 1950, then later on he started to have a garden in the back because he had all that acreage.

232
00:19:32.662 --> 00:19:34.345
<v COOPER>So around 1950?

233
00:19:34.345 --> 00:19:37.709
<v SHELTON>That's when we built the house.

234
00:19:37.709 --> 00:19:42.757
<v COOPER>And what sorts of plants were in the garden?

235
00:19:42.757 --> 00:19:45.000
Do you remember?

236
00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:46.193
<v SHELTON>What vegetables?

237
00:19:46.193 --> 00:19:50.964
Tomatoes and peas, because my daddy loved peas.

238
00:19:50.964 --> 00:19:54.542
He didn't eat any dry beans.

239
00:19:54.542 --> 00:19:59.909
Everything always had to be fresh, especially fresh peas.

240
00:19:59.909 --> 00:20:09.451
As a matter of fact, we didn't have any beans and rice until we started school.

241
00:20:09.451 --> 00:20:18.396
My brothers especially, because my mother would always have . . . well not dried beans and rice;

242
00:20:18.396 --> 00:20:29.727
they'd have fresh beans and fresh peas because they would either gather from the little garden that they grew,

243
00:20:29.727 --> 00:20:38.076
or from my grandfather who lived in Bancroft, because he gardened for his family.

244
00:20:38.076 --> 00:20:42.847
It wasn't for selling and all of that.

245
00:20:42.847 --> 00:20:46.425
He did it for his children.

246
00:20:46.425 --> 00:20:54.178
<v COOPER>Was there another place in Mossville that your parents would buy food from?

247
00:20:54.178 --> 00:21:03.124
<v SHELTON>They'd go to Westlake to . . . because Westlake was the town, you might as well say.

248
00:21:03.124 --> 00:21:08.491
They'd go there and buy groceries that they needed.

249
00:21:08.491 --> 00:21:14.455
Such groceries that they did buy, that's where they'd go.

250
00:21:14.455 --> 00:21:24.593
I remember even going to Westlake and buying . . . that's where we'd get some of our clothing too.

251
00:21:24.593 --> 00:21:27.575
My mother was a seamstress.

252
00:21:27.575 --> 00:21:32.345
They'd buy the flour in the flour sacks.

253
00:21:32.345 --> 00:21:59.182
She'd always keep the sacks and take them loose and they'd try . . . she'd always try to buy the same kind . . . the same design flour sack so that when she got ready to make something for us to wear she'd have enough pieces to put together.

254
00:21:59.182 --> 00:22:12.898
Let's say if she had three sacks of flour, all those sacks would have the same stripes or flowers or whatever on them.

255
00:22:12.898 --> 00:22:15.880
So that's what we did.

256
00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:22.440
I remember we'd even get the little shoes there in Westlake.

257
00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:29.000
<v COOPER>And where did you buy kerosene from for the lamps?

258
00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:35.677
<v SHELTON>I don't know where they bought the kerosene.

259
00:22:35.677 --> 00:22:45.693
I guess in Westlake because [nowhere?] in Mossville they could find any.

260
00:22:45.693 --> 00:23:04.055
<v COOPER>I want to talk a little bit more about the geographic town of Mossville and what it looked like physically growing up.

261
00:23:04.055 --> 00:23:14.906
What . . . As far as you know, what are the geographic boundaries of Mossville?

262
00:23:14.906 --> 00:23:24.087
<v SHELTON>From the school . . . You mean where it started from the school?

263
00:23:24.087 --> 00:23:29.929
I don't know how far it went.

264
00:23:29.929 --> 00:23:34.102
I can't estimate that part.

265
00:23:34.102 --> 00:23:46.622
All I know is it extended from the school . . . You know where Mossville School is?

266
00:23:46.622 --> 00:23:47.457
Okay.

267
00:23:47.457 --> 00:23:58.307
From there on to South to West . . . It was North, South, East, West.

268
00:23:58.307 --> 00:24:04.150
I can't recall the boundaries of it.

269
00:24:04.150 --> 00:24:05.819
<v COOPER>That's okay.

270
00:24:05.819 --> 00:24:15.000
And how has the community changed physically within your lifetime?

271
00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:21.977
<v SHELTON>Well, we didn't have running water and sewage back there then.

272
00:24:21.977 --> 00:24:30.856
As I've gotten older the town has gotten running water, it has gotten sewage.

273
00:24:30.856 --> 00:24:35.930
Oh, I know one thing we didn't have.

274
00:24:35.930 --> 00:24:47.346
We had a two-room school back there then when I was a youngster growing up in grade school.

275
00:24:47.346 --> 00:24:51.152
It was a two room school.

276
00:24:51.152 --> 00:25:07.008
The school had . . . We could play underneath it because it was up on stilts and it had a porch all the way across the front.

277
00:25:07.008 --> 00:25:13.984
They had what they call coat . . . They call them cloak rooms,

278
00:25:13.984 --> 00:25:26.035
but the coat rooms where if you get in trouble, the teacher might put you in the coat room.

279
00:25:26.035 --> 00:25:33.646
Then we had . . . Like I said, we had the two-room school room.

280
00:25:33.646 --> 00:25:44.428
Later on, another building was added and it was a one-room building, but it housed several grades.

281
00:25:44.428 --> 00:25:48.233
I don't know . . . four, five, six.

282
00:25:48.233 --> 00:25:59.650
But the first one, you were in there from first grade all the way up to fifth grade.

283
00:25:59.650 --> 00:26:04.089
That was basically how the school looked.

284
00:26:04.089 --> 00:26:14.872
I'm trying to see what I have here on this sheet that I remember from that building.

285
00:26:14.872 --> 00:26:18.677
And the teachers were very strict.

286
00:26:18.677 --> 00:26:28.191
They were very concerned about the students learning back there in that little two-room school.

287
00:26:28.191 --> 00:26:40.875
We also ended up gradually getting our own cafeteria, because before all of us had to bring our sack lunches.

288
00:26:40.875 --> 00:26:47.218
We'd go underneath the building and share our sack lunches.

289
00:26:47.218 --> 00:26:58.000
The play was . . . You hardly ever . . . I don’t ever recall kids fighting or anything back then.

290
00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:00.769
<v COOPER>And would you play with the neighbor kids back at home too?

291
00:27:00.769 --> 00:27:01.231
<v SHELTON>Oh, yeah.

292
00:27:01.231 --> 00:27:02.615
<v COOPER>Do you have any memories of . . .

293
00:27:02.615 --> 00:27:03.538
<v SHELTON>Playing with the neighbors’ . . .

294
00:27:03.538 --> 00:27:04.000
<v COOPER>[Agrees]

295
00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:04.622
<v SHELTON>. . . kids?

296
00:27:04.622 --> 00:27:12.085
Oh yes, a lot of memories of playing with the neighbors' kids.

297
00:27:12.085 --> 00:27:21.415
Like I said, we were seemingly the younger ones on Prater Road at the time.

298
00:27:21.415 --> 00:27:37.585
We really liked when it rained [laughs] for some reason because we could get . . . we were able to get out there and wade in the ponds.

299
00:27:37.585 --> 00:27:41.317
If the water was deep enough,

300
00:27:41.317 --> 00:27:59.976
the guys would carry us on their shoulder and they'd play in the water and throw us in the water because they were much bigger than we are . . . we were.

301
00:27:59.976 --> 00:28:04.951
We always had . . . oh, what [do my?] say?

302
00:28:04.951 --> 00:28:13.037
The fun that we had was never anything that was derogatory or anything.

303
00:28:13.037 --> 00:28:19.878
I mean we'd sometimes make up our own games and play.

304
00:28:19.878 --> 00:28:24.854
We didn't have baseball bats at that time.

305
00:28:24.854 --> 00:28:32.939
We might get a broom handle and use that for the baseball bat.

306
00:28:32.939 --> 00:28:42.890
We'd play jump rope because we would get a jump rope maybe for Christmas or something.

307
00:28:42.890 --> 00:28:46.000
We played jump rope.

308
00:28:46.000 --> 00:28:49.354
Oh, and another thing I remember.

309
00:28:49.354 --> 00:28:57.740
We made . . . The girls, my sister and I, would make doll houses out of boxes.

310
00:28:57.740 --> 00:29:05.008
She always wanted to be an architect, but she didn't go into that.

311
00:29:05.008 --> 00:29:20.661
But anyway, we want . . . we would make doll houses out of boxes because our parents couldn't afford to buy the little doll houses that they had back then.

312
00:29:20.661 --> 00:29:26.811
From the magazines, they had the old [?] and Montgomery Ward magazines.

313
00:29:26.811 --> 00:29:44.142
But we'd cut pictures from out of the magazines and put them on the back of a stick and those would be the people that lived in the house and whatnot.

314
00:29:44.142 --> 00:29:57.000
We would make dresses out of the paper bags and whatnot to put on these little people for our doll houses. [laughs]

315
00:29:57.000 --> 00:30:01.288
<v COOPER>And do you remember who your neighbors were?

316
00:30:01.288 --> 00:30:03.967
<v SHELTON>[Agrees] We had the Praters.

317
00:30:03.967 --> 00:30:10.399
We had the Hartmans, which was Dorothy and her sisters and brothers.

318
00:30:10.399 --> 00:30:15.222
We had the Moutons down the street from us.

319
00:30:15.222 --> 00:30:20.046
We had the Wilmores across the street from us.

320
00:30:20.046 --> 00:30:25.405
We had the Fontenots down the street from us too.

321
00:30:25.405 --> 00:30:31.301
Those were about the only ones that I . . . Oh, the Jourdans.

322
00:30:31.301 --> 00:30:36.124
Those were the ones when I was growing up.

323
00:30:36.124 --> 00:30:42.556
Later on there were other families to move in on Prater Road.

324
00:30:42.556 --> 00:30:46.843
<v COOPER>Did you have any relatives that lived nearby?

325
00:30:46.843 --> 00:30:54.346
<v SHELTON>I had one uncle, and he lived on Old Spanish Trail . . . my daddy's uncle.

326
00:30:54.346 --> 00:31:00.242
Other than that we didn't have any other relatives in Mossville.

327
00:31:00.242 --> 00:31:05.601
<v COOPER>Were there ever any occasions for family gatherings in Mossville?

328
00:31:05.601 --> 00:31:08.817
<v SHELTON>Like a family reunion or anything?

329
00:31:08.817 --> 00:31:10.961
<v COOPER>Yeah, or a wedding.

330
00:31:10.961 --> 00:31:19.000
Did you ever have weddings, or funerals, or maybe a certain holiday in Mossville?

331
00:31:19.000 --> 00:31:25.791
<v SHELTON>Well, we would always go and spend the holidays with our grandparents.

332
00:31:25.791 --> 00:31:38.242
Now there were funerals there in Mossville, but there were not relatives’ funerals or anything because our relatives were away just about.

333
00:31:38.242 --> 00:31:43.335
<v COOPER>What do you remember about the funerals in Mossville?

334
00:31:43.335 --> 00:31:46.731
<v SHELTON>That was like a family reunion.

335
00:31:46.731 --> 00:31:55.786
People that you had not seen in a long while, you see them at the funeral.

336
00:31:55.786 --> 00:32:02.011
That was about it because I remember at . . . just like now.

337
00:32:02.011 --> 00:32:08.236
“Oh, I don't see Harry until there's a funeral or whatever.”

338
00:32:08.236 --> 00:32:11.632
But that's all I can recall.

339
00:32:11.632 --> 00:32:16.159
Oh, I know one thing about the funerals.

340
00:32:16.159 --> 00:32:22.951
They had the wakes in the houses instead of a funeral home.

341
00:32:22.951 --> 00:32:27.478
The wakes were always there in the house.

342
00:32:27.478 --> 00:32:39.363
But of course, I never had the privilege of sleeping in the same house where a body had been overnight [laughs].

343
00:32:39.363 --> 00:32:45.588
I don't know if that would have been weird or what.

344
00:32:45.588 --> 00:32:48.418
I never had that privilege.

345
00:32:48.418 --> 00:32:55.209
Some of my friends have had it, but we didn't have it.

346
00:32:55.209 --> 00:32:57.473
I didn't have it.

347
00:32:57.473 --> 00:33:02.000
<v COOPER>Was there a funeral home in Mossville?

348
00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:03.867
<v SHELTON>No. No funeral home.

349
00:33:03.867 --> 00:33:08.533
<v COOPER>So the post office for Mossville was established in 1915.

350
00:33:08.533 --> 00:33:13.200
Do you know how long it was in operation for?

351
00:33:13.200 --> 00:33:15.533
<v SHELTON>A post office you said?

352
00:33:15.533 --> 00:33:16.000
1915?

353
00:33:16.000 --> 00:33:20.200
Well I don't know about the post office [laughs].

354
00:33:20.200 --> 00:33:23.467
I don't know about the post office.

355
00:33:23.467 --> 00:33:24.867
<v COOPER>That's okay [laughs].

356
00:33:24.867 --> 00:33:25.333
<v SHELTON>Okay.

357
00:33:25.333 --> 00:33:29.067
<v COOPER>I figured I would ask just in case.

358
00:33:29.067 --> 00:33:30.000
Was . . .

359
00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:34.808
<v SHELTON>Somebody else supposed to mention about a post office?

360
00:33:34.808 --> 00:33:36.945
<v COOPER>I think so, yeah.

361
00:33:36.945 --> 00:33:42.288
<v SHELTON>Well I never . . . I didn't ever know we had one.

362
00:33:42.288 --> 00:33:48.164
<v COOPER>Do you know if Mossville was ever incorporated into a town?

363
00:33:48.164 --> 00:33:50.301
<v SHELTON>I don't think so.

364
00:33:50.301 --> 00:33:51.904
I don't recall.

365
00:33:51.904 --> 00:33:58.849
I'd have to ask my husband if he . . . which, he's not from Mossville.

366
00:33:58.849 --> 00:34:04.192
He's from Westlake, so I don't know if he knows.

367
00:34:04.192 --> 00:34:10.603
So I don't know if he knows if it was ever incorporated.

368
00:34:10.603 --> 00:34:12.740
I don't think so.

369
00:34:12.740 --> 00:34:17.548
I don't recall, because I left there in '58.

370
00:34:17.548 --> 00:34:26.096
Yeah, I left in '58 . . . and came back for. . . '58, '59 . . . [Shelton clarifies it was 1962].

371
00:34:26.096 --> 00:34:33.575
I think I stayed there four years and then from there I came here.

372
00:34:33.575 --> 00:34:42.658
<v COOPER>And you mentioned when you first lived in Mossville that there wasn't running water, sewage, or electricity.

373
00:34:42.658 --> 00:34:48.000
Do you remember when you first got those amenities?

374
00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:50.852
<v SHELTON>No, I sure don't recall.

375
00:34:50.852 --> 00:34:54.845
I don't recall when we got electricity.

376
00:34:54.845 --> 00:34:56.556
<v COOPER>[sneezes] Excuse me.

377
00:34:56.556 --> 00:35:00.549
<v SHELTON>I can't recall when we got them.

378
00:35:00.549 --> 00:35:07.394
<v COOPER>Do you remember what it was like when you did get them?

379
00:35:07.394 --> 00:35:11.958
<v SHELTON>Yeah. [?] when we got . . . I'm trying to recall.

380
00:35:11.958 --> 00:35:15.951
Really, my memory is fading there too.

381
00:35:15.951 --> 00:35:26.218
All I can remember when my . . . when we got the first television there in our city [Shelton’s edit:

382
00:35:26.218 --> 00:35:34.204
town] . . . and I said it, that was when the Rigmaidens got the first television.

383
00:35:34.204 --> 00:35:39.338
That's what I can remember, having that first television,

384
00:35:39.338 --> 00:35:50.746
because we all went down there to watch TV that Sunday, a particular Sunday on the black and white TV.

385
00:35:50.746 --> 00:35:57.592
But I can't recall when it was when we got the electricity.

386
00:35:57.592 --> 00:36:02.725
I know it had to be a joyous occasion.

387
00:36:02.725 --> 00:36:09.000
But so far as being able to recall, I can't.

388
00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:16.309
<v COOPER>And what else . . . I know you mentioned in your first interview about Lula's Canteen.

389
00:36:16.309 --> 00:36:22.051
Can you describe a little bit about what that was like?

390
00:36:22.051 --> 00:36:23.618
<v SHELTON>Ms. Lula's Canteen?

391
00:36:23.618 --> 00:36:24.140
<v COOPER>Yeah.

392
00:36:24.140 --> 00:36:24.662
<v SHELTON>Okay.

393
00:36:24.662 --> 00:36:36.147
Her canteen was in the front of her house, and that was the place where the youngsters would go on the weekends.

394
00:36:36.147 --> 00:36:41.890
We'd dance and have candy and sodas, and that's about it.

395
00:36:41.890 --> 00:36:42.934
Clean fun.

396
00:36:42.934 --> 00:36:46.066
Meet our little boyfriends and girlfriends.

397
00:36:46.066 --> 00:36:50.765
<v COOPER>And what kind of music would you listen to?

398
00:36:50.765 --> 00:36:56.507
<v SHELTON>I guess you'd call it rock and roll back there then.

399
00:36:56.507 --> 00:37:03.294
But I can't recall the titles of any of them back there then.

400
00:37:03.294 --> 00:37:13.735
All I can recall is getting back there and dancing, getting up there on that floor dancing, and jigging, whatever.

401
00:37:13.735 --> 00:37:14.779
That's all.

402
00:37:14.779 --> 00:37:20.000
<v COOPER>And did you go to the drive in movies?

403
00:37:20.000 --> 00:37:26.212
<v SHELTON>[Agrees] after I got to be a teenager with my boyfriend. [laughs]

404
00:37:26.212 --> 00:37:30.871
<v COOPER>What do you remember about the drive in movies?

405
00:37:30.871 --> 00:37:39.153
<v SHELTON>That we'd drive up there and they'd have the big screen out there on Highway 90.

406
00:37:39.153 --> 00:37:40.188
We'd watch.

407
00:37:40.188 --> 00:37:43.812
That's all I can remember from it.

408
00:37:43.812 --> 00:37:44.329
<v COOPER>Okay.

409
00:37:44.329 --> 00:37:50.541
And also, I think either you or Dorothy mentioned The Paradise Club . . .

410
00:37:50.541 --> 00:37:52.094
<v SHELTON>The Paradise Club . . .

411
00:37:52.094 --> 00:37:53.647
<v COOPER>. . . or Montgomery's Club.

412
00:37:53.647 --> 00:37:58.824
<v SHELTON>Oh, Montgomery's Club was down the street from our house.

413
00:37:58.824 --> 00:37:59.859
<v COOPER>Oh yeah?

414
00:37:59.859 --> 00:38:04.000
Could you describe what that was like?

415
00:38:04.000 --> 00:38:08.257
<v SHELTON>Oh, they were boogieing all the time [laughs].

416
00:38:08.257 --> 00:38:13.046
We were forbidden to go down there as children.

417
00:38:13.046 --> 00:38:14.642
We couldn't go.

418
00:38:14.642 --> 00:38:23.156
We could pass by, but so far as going in there, no. That was for adults.

419
00:38:23.156 --> 00:38:27.413
We could hear the music from our house.

420
00:38:27.413 --> 00:38:33.798
We'd sit out there on the porch and listen to the music.

421
00:38:33.798 --> 00:38:36.459
It was just that loud.

422
00:38:36.459 --> 00:38:39.119
<v COOPER>Did your parents ever go?

423
00:38:39.119 --> 00:38:39.651
<v SHELTON>No.

424
00:38:39.651 --> 00:38:42.844
<v COOPER>Do you know who owned it?

425
00:38:42.844 --> 00:38:43.376
<v SHELTON>Huh?

426
00:38:43.376 --> 00:38:46.569
<v COOPER>Do you know who owned it?

427
00:38:46.569 --> 00:38:48.165
<v SHELTON>Yes, Valery Montgomery.

428
00:38:48.165 --> 00:38:53.486
My daddy, they were religious folks and they didn't go.

429
00:38:53.486 --> 00:39:00.936
<v COOPER>I think on your bio form it said that your father's name . . . was Reverend.

430
00:39:00.936 --> 00:39:02.000
<v SHELTON>Yes.

431
00:39:02.000 --> 00:39:04.840
<v COOPER>So he was a reverend.

432
00:39:04.840 --> 00:39:05.407
<v SHELTON>Yes.

433
00:39:05.407 --> 00:39:08.247
<v COOPER>Which church was he at?

434
00:39:08.247 --> 00:39:11.086
<v SHELTON>Rose of Sharon Sanctified Church.

435
00:39:11.086 --> 00:39:13.926
That church is demolished now.

436
00:39:13.926 --> 00:39:19.037
Well, practically everything else out there is demolished now.

437
00:39:19.037 --> 00:39:24.148
<v COOPER>And how was your mom involved in the church?

438
00:39:24.148 --> 00:39:26.420
<v SHELTON>She would take us.

439
00:39:26.420 --> 00:39:33.235
Sometimes if they had a secretary of the church, and the deaconess,

440
00:39:33.235 --> 00:39:41.185
and she was that . . . she kept herself busy doing something there at the church.

441
00:39:41.185 --> 00:39:41.753
Yeah.

442
00:39:41.753 --> 00:39:46.864
<v COOPER>Did you have any jobs growing up in Mossville?

443
00:39:46.864 --> 00:39:48.000
No?

444
00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:51.880
<v SHELTON>No other than around the house [laughs].

445
00:39:51.880 --> 00:40:00.194
<v COOPER>Okay, in your first interview you and Dorothy . . . you spoke a little bit about gardening,

446
00:40:00.194 --> 00:40:05.182
and your upbringing, and you talked about cane, sugarcane.

447
00:40:05.182 --> 00:40:17.930
Was there any growing in your father's garden or in the neighborhood for plants for home remedies, or any sort of medical treatment?

448
00:40:17.930 --> 00:40:19.593
<v SHELTON>No, not really.

449
00:40:19.593 --> 00:40:31.787
I remember my dad going to my . . . his father-in-law's, my mother's dad house to get roots, sassafras roots, and they'd use that.

450
00:40:31.787 --> 00:40:40.101
But other than that, I don't . . . I know we didn't do any from our house.

451
00:40:40.101 --> 00:40:46.198
Oh, I do remember one thing, come to think of it.

452
00:40:46.198 --> 00:40:47.306
Pine needles.

453
00:40:47.306 --> 00:40:57.283
They'd get the pine needles and boil those pine needles and make, I guess, some kind of liquid,

454
00:40:57.283 --> 00:41:06.151
and have us to drink that when we would have a cold or something and fever.

455
00:41:06.151 --> 00:41:18.345
Put lemon juice, they'd put . . . squeeze lemon in it and they'd have us to drink that spoonful’s of it at a time.

456
00:41:18.345 --> 00:41:20.008
But pine needles.

457
00:41:20.008 --> 00:41:26.659
My husband even tried that on me [laughs] a few years ago.

458
00:41:26.659 --> 00:41:28.322
I was sick.

459
00:41:28.322 --> 00:41:39.961
He said, “Oh I’m going to go . . . I know what I want to do [ . . .?] get some pine needles and boil them.”

460
00:41:39.961 --> 00:41:41.624
But, they worked.

461
00:41:41.624 --> 00:41:42.733
They worked.

462
00:41:42.733 --> 00:41:46.058
They had to be green, though.

463
00:41:46.058 --> 00:41:47.167
Green pine.

464
00:41:47.167 --> 00:41:58.252
I guess it's that turpentine that was in the . . . in those pine needles that they were getting out of them.

465
00:41:58.252 --> 00:42:04.903
That was the only home remedies that I can recall back there.

466
00:42:04.903 --> 00:42:11.000
<v COOPER>And where would your family go to get medical treatment?

467
00:42:11.000 --> 00:42:19.000
<v SHELTON>They had . . . The doctors would come and go to your house at that time.

468
00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:28.714
We had an old doctor, Dr. Ross, and that's who would doctor on my parents and us.

469
00:42:28.714 --> 00:42:33.286
And for having those babies, they'd have midwives.

470
00:42:33.286 --> 00:42:41.857
<v COOPER>Did your mom do an in-home birth for when she gave birth to your siblings?

471
00:42:41.857 --> 00:42:44.714
<v SHELTON>Did she do any what?

472
00:42:44.714 --> 00:42:47.000
<v COOPER>An in-home birth?

473
00:42:47.000 --> 00:42:50.564
<v SHELTON>Not that . . . I guess she did,

474
00:42:50.564 --> 00:43:00.069
but I can't recall . . . because I know we had to . . . we were delivered by a midwife,

475
00:43:00.069 --> 00:43:06.604
my sister and I, and maybe my baby sister was too.

476
00:43:06.604 --> 00:43:19.079
But I was too young to remember my baby sister being born at home, but I know she had to be.

477
00:43:19.079 --> 00:43:27.990
But I know my brothers were born in the hospital because they came along later.

478
00:43:27.990 --> 00:43:33.337
The three brothers were born in St. Pat's Hospital.

479
00:43:33.337 --> 00:43:39.277
<v COOPER>Do you remember any of the names of the midwives?

480
00:43:39.277 --> 00:43:40.465
<v SHELTON>[Disagrees] [laughs]

481
00:43:40.465 --> 00:43:47.000
<v COOPER>Do you . . . What do you remember about the sugarcane mill?

482
00:43:47.000 --> 00:43:48.366
<v SHELTON>The sugarcane mill?

483
00:43:48.366 --> 00:43:52.463
Well, I don't know anything about a sugarcane mill.

484
00:43:52.463 --> 00:43:57.016
All I know, Dorothy was mentioning about a sugarcane mill.

485
00:43:57.016 --> 00:44:00.659
I don't know anything about a sugarcane mill.

486
00:44:00.659 --> 00:44:06.577
All I know that my daddy would get sugarcane and . . . from my grandfather.

487
00:44:06.577 --> 00:44:18.870
He'd sit there and peel the skin off of them and then cut it up into little bitty pieces and give each one of the kids that.

488
00:44:18.870 --> 00:44:27.520
That's the only thing I can remember about the sugarcane, other than my grandfather making syrup from the sugarcane.

489
00:44:27.520 --> 00:44:36.626
But so far as having any at our house, the only sugarcane we had at our house was the sticks.

490
00:44:36.626 --> 00:44:37.992
We'd get those.

491
00:44:37.992 --> 00:44:43.000
<v COOPER>And what would your grandfather use the sugarcane syrup for?

492
00:44:43.000 --> 00:44:46.706
<v SHELTON>For his biscuits and our biscuits [laughs].

493
00:44:46.706 --> 00:44:52.529
Yeah, it's just like . . . it tastes just like the Steen’s syrup.

494
00:44:52.529 --> 00:44:58.353
<v COOPER>Do you know anything, or remember anything, about the oil refineries?

495
00:44:58.353 --> 00:45:05.765
<v SHELTON>Other than my daddy worked there and . . . Quite often they would have a release.

496
00:45:05.765 --> 00:45:08.941
We didn't know it was release.

497
00:45:08.941 --> 00:45:14.235
They'd have a release and a big old booming sound.

498
00:45:14.235 --> 00:45:17.412
I can remember the booming sound.

499
00:45:17.412 --> 00:45:24.824
And every time they'd have that booming sound, we'd say, "Oh, Daddy's gotten killed,"

500
00:45:24.824 --> 00:45:32.235
because it was so loud and we thought it was an explosion each time.

501
00:45:32.235 --> 00:45:39.647
But it was not; it was those . . . that release that they . . . At the time,

502
00:45:39.647 --> 00:45:43.353
we didn't know it was a release.

503
00:45:43.353 --> 00:45:47.059
We didn't know nothing about a release.

504
00:45:47.059 --> 00:45:54.471
But now that I've gotten older, I know that's what it was, a release.

505
00:45:54.471 --> 00:46:02.941
And they'd have . . . Every year they would give a picnic for the employees and their families.

506
00:46:02.941 --> 00:46:09.294
They'd also give a Christmas party for the employees and their families.

507
00:46:09.294 --> 00:46:13.529
And all the children would get a gift.

508
00:46:13.529 --> 00:46:17.235
So that's what I remember about that.

509
00:46:17.235 --> 00:46:22.000
<v COOPER>Do you remember what gift they gave you?

510
00:46:22.000 --> 00:46:27.335
<v SHELTON>Oh, well I know you'd always get fruit which was good.

511
00:46:27.335 --> 00:46:37.520
You'd always get fruit and some kind of little . . . You know, I can't remember what they gave me back there then.

512
00:46:37.520 --> 00:46:43.340
<v COOPER>Where would they hold the parties for Christmas and for the picnics?

513
00:46:43.340 --> 00:46:49.645
<v SHELTON>They had a big building you know Cities Service was rich back then.

514
00:46:49.645 --> 00:46:54.980
And they had a big meeting room where they'd have that.

515
00:46:54.980 --> 00:47:08.560
For the picnics, they had even a big area where it was covered for where they would do the barbequing and what not and the cooking underneath there.

516
00:47:08.560 --> 00:47:17.290
But you could use . . . They had seating areas all over because it was a park more or less.

517
00:47:17.290 --> 00:47:22.625
But it was their park that they used for their employees.

518
00:47:22.625 --> 00:47:23.110
<v COOPER>Okay.

519
00:47:23.110 --> 00:47:33.780
So, to ask you a little bit about family life, how were decisions made in your family when you were growing up?

520
00:47:33.780 --> 00:47:39.115
<v SHELTON>Well, my mom and my daddy always made their decisions together.

521
00:47:39.115 --> 00:47:51.240
If my dad would come up with a decision he’d pass it by my mom to see if she approved of it, and vice versa.

522
00:47:51.240 --> 00:47:54.150
That's the way it's always been.

523
00:47:54.150 --> 00:47:59.000
<v COOPER>And who made decisions about housekeeping and the budget?

524
00:47:59.000 --> 00:48:09.708
<v SHELTON>I think my mom did more of that, because I remember my dad coming home and he'd always give her his check.

525
00:48:09.708 --> 00:48:15.062
She mostly did that . . . took care of that part of it.

526
00:48:15.062 --> 00:48:19.929
<v COOPER>Was she also in charge of child care and discipline?

527
00:48:19.929 --> 00:48:20.416
<v SHELTON>[Agrees]

528
00:48:20.416 --> 00:48:26.257
<v COOPER>And was it the same when you were raising your family, too?

529
00:48:26.257 --> 00:48:28.690
Do you feel like you . . .?

530
00:48:28.690 --> 00:48:29.177
<v SHELTON>Huh?

531
00:48:29.177 --> 00:48:35.018
<v COOPER>Was it the same when you were raising your family as well?

532
00:48:35.018 --> 00:48:42.805
Do you feel like it was . . . that you parented in the same way you were parented?

533
00:48:42.805 --> 00:48:46.212
<v SHELTON>Well, I tried to for a while.

534
00:48:46.212 --> 00:48:55.460
But my husband, he seemed to have been better . . . not so far as the housekeeping and all that part.

535
00:48:55.460 --> 00:49:01.301
Decorating the house and all that, that was left up to me.

536
00:49:01.301 --> 00:49:12.982
But when it came to financing, paying for the house and putting money aside, he would be responsible for that investing and all that.

537
00:49:12.982 --> 00:49:15.903
He's over that part of it.

538
00:49:15.903 --> 00:49:19.796
But so far as the . . . all the other,

539
00:49:19.796 --> 00:49:28.558
the cooking and figuring out what kind of furniture and all that stuff, I'm really responsible for that.

540
00:49:28.558 --> 00:49:37.319
But we . . . I'd always pass it by him, "Do you like this?" or, "Is it okay with you?"

541
00:49:37.319 --> 00:49:38.292
Like that.

542
00:49:38.292 --> 00:49:49.000
We'd try to share, but like I said, he's over the financial part of it and I do the other part.

543
00:49:49.000 --> 00:49:59.845
<v COOPER>So you said . . . I remember you said that you didn't do any cooking growing up because you weren't allowed in the kitchen.

544
00:49:59.845 --> 00:50:00.338
<v SHELTON>Huh?

545
00:50:00.338 --> 00:50:01.324
<v COOPER>You didn't . . .

546
00:50:01.324 --> 00:50:02.310
<v SHELTON>Oh, no.

547
00:50:02.310 --> 00:50:08.225
<v COOPER>But what sorts . . . what did you learn from your parents growing up?

548
00:50:08.225 --> 00:50:16.113
Did you learn how . . . Were there certain dishes that they did teach you how to cook?

549
00:50:16.113 --> 00:50:22.521
Or did they teach you how to do other things around the house?

550
00:50:22.521 --> 00:50:29.915
<v SHELTON>They taught me how to keep up the house . . . try to keep up the house.

551
00:50:29.915 --> 00:50:35.338
They taught us how to try to conserve food, don't waste.

552
00:50:35.338 --> 00:50:38.789
That part, they taught us that part.

553
00:50:38.789 --> 00:50:45.690
I've always tried not to waste and to . . . I mean [do?] have any waste.

554
00:50:45.690 --> 00:50:48.648
So that, they taught us that.

555
00:50:48.648 --> 00:50:55.056
And, "Always have a bird in hand,” is what they would always say.

556
00:50:55.056 --> 00:50:59.000
<v COOPER>What food today brings back childhood memories?

557
00:50:59.000 --> 00:51:00.349
<v SHELTON>Oh, Lord.

558
00:51:00.349 --> 00:51:09.791
Macaroni and cheese and fried chicken, because every Sunday seemingly we had fried chicken.

559
00:51:09.791 --> 00:51:17.209
<v COOPER>And what was your mother's favorite dish to make growing up?

560
00:51:17.209 --> 00:51:21.256
<v SHELTON>Mother was such a good cook.

561
00:51:21.256 --> 00:51:28.000
For dessert, pineapple coconut cake was her favorite for making.

562
00:51:28.000 --> 00:51:30.698
For vegetables, collard greens.

563
00:51:30.698 --> 00:51:36.767
She liked to cook collard greens and mustard greens.

564
00:51:36.767 --> 00:51:40.140
Those were her two favorites.

565
00:51:40.140 --> 00:51:41.488
And cornbread.

566
00:51:41.488 --> 00:51:47.558
My daddy had to have hot bread every day.

567
00:51:47.558 --> 00:51:49.581
He wanted cornbread.

568
00:51:49.581 --> 00:51:54.302
So we ate a lot of cornbread.

569
00:51:54.302 --> 00:52:09.814
Now, he didn't want it cooked on top of the stove like they usually . . . I know some people call it hot water cornbread.

570
00:52:09.814 --> 00:52:15.884
I don't know if you've ever heard of it.

571
00:52:15.884 --> 00:52:18.581
You're mighty young [laughs].

572
00:52:18.581 --> 00:52:26.674
They'd put it in the skillet and cook the cornbread on top.

573
00:52:26.674 --> 00:52:32.744
Daddy had to have his baked in the oven.

574
00:52:32.744 --> 00:52:36.791
<v COOPER>Did he have any other stipulations?

575
00:52:36.791 --> 00:52:44.884
Like I know your grandfather would only eat on a white plate.

576
00:52:44.884 --> 00:52:45.558
<v SHELTON>No . . .

577
00:52:45.558 --> 00:52:48.256
<v COOPER>Was there anything else?

578
00:52:48.256 --> 00:52:55.000
<v SHELTON>. . . Daddy wasn't . . . he wasn't persnickety like Papa David. [laughs]

579
00:52:55.000 --> 00:52:59.950
<v COOPER>And did they . . . did your mom pass any recipes on to you?

580
00:52:59.950 --> 00:53:03.662
<v SHELTON>No. Seemed like everything was always in the head.

581
00:53:03.662 --> 00:53:11.500
They'd take a little bit of this and a little bit of that and a little bit of that.

582
00:53:11.500 --> 00:53:12.738
"Well how much . . . ?"

583
00:53:12.738 --> 00:53:18.925
Just put a little bit in there," like Justin Wilson used to do on TV.

584
00:53:18.925 --> 00:53:21.812
That's the way they would do it.

585
00:53:21.812 --> 00:53:30.888
It would always come out just fine, but you couldn't go behind them and try to do it like they did it.

586
00:53:30.888 --> 00:53:33.362
That was out of the question.

587
00:53:33.362 --> 00:53:38.725
<v COOPER>Do you cook anything now, today that she used to cook back then?

588
00:53:38.725 --> 00:53:42.025
<v SHELTON>Spaghetti and cheese . . . because my grandbaby loves pasta.

589
00:53:42.025 --> 00:53:44.088
So, spaghetti and cheese is . . .

590
00:53:44.088 --> 00:53:53.988
<v COOPER>Were there special foods that were cooked for special events or family reunions that you only got at a certain time of the year?

591
00:53:53.988 --> 00:53:54.812
<v SHELTON>Oh, dumplings.

592
00:53:54.812 --> 00:53:56.875
Chicken dumplings and squirrel dumplings.

593
00:53:56.875 --> 00:54:01.000
I don't like squirrel dumplings. [also fruitcake, Shelton adds]

594
00:54:01.000 --> 00:54:03.337
<v COOPER>When were those made?

595
00:54:03.337 --> 00:54:06.843
<v SHELTON>During . . . when it would get cold.

596
00:54:06.843 --> 00:54:14.439
But when we'd have special family things that the chicken dumplings would be . . .

597
00:54:14.439 --> 00:54:15.024
<v COOPER>Okay.

598
00:54:15.024 --> 00:54:23.788
I have a few more questions about Mossville and, specifically, Civil Rights history in Mossville.

599
00:54:23.788 --> 00:54:32.553
Some people mentioned the importance of voting, especially after the Voting Rights Act in 1965.

600
00:54:32.553 --> 00:54:37.812
So you would have been about twenty-five around them.

601
00:54:37.812 --> 00:54:46.576
Do you have any memories of voter registration in the schools, or voting in Mossville?

602
00:54:46.576 --> 00:54:57.094
<v SHELTON>When I started voting . . . The Civil Rights Movement took place when I was in school back in 1960.

603
00:54:57.094 --> 00:55:02.353
I was in Baton Rouge [Louisiana] at the time.

604
00:55:02.353 --> 00:55:11.702
I remember . . . Now how . . . What was happening back in Mossville during that time, I don't know.

605
00:55:11.702 --> 00:55:30.984
All I remember when we were . . . in 1960 when we had a march on the capitol as a student and we were all told to leave the campus and go to the capitol.

606
00:55:30.984 --> 00:55:42.086
Being a little country girl from Mossville, I joined them, [laughs] but they put the dogs on us and [. . . ?].

607
00:55:42.086 --> 00:55:48.514
So we had to end up going back to the campus.

608
00:55:48.514 --> 00:55:54.941
That was all I can remember about that Civil Rights Movement.

609
00:55:54.941 --> 00:56:07.212
But I do know that from that time on, we were able to vote. [Register to vote?] From that time on,

610
00:56:07.212 --> 00:56:15.392
I started voting . . . because I finished back in '62, so I started voting then.

611
00:56:15.392 --> 00:56:28.831
<v COOPER>In your first interview you mentioned that for your first year of high school you had to go to Washington because of segregation.

612
00:56:28.831 --> 00:56:30.000
<v SHELTON>Right.

613
00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:38.657
<v COOPER>Do you have any memories about what that was like growing up during segregation in Mossville?

614
00:56:38.657 --> 00:56:48.937
<v SHELTON>We had to get up real early to catch the bus to go to the end of the street.

615
00:56:48.937 --> 00:56:53.266
I lived in the middle of Prater Road,

616
00:56:53.266 --> 00:57:06.792
so we had to go to the end of Prater Road . . . north end near the Spanish Trail to catch the bus to go to Washington.

617
00:57:06.792 --> 00:57:13.826
Eventually, they started dropping us off on Prater Road, directly on Prater Road.

618
00:57:13.826 --> 00:57:20.319
That's what I remember, going, getting up early and getting home late.

619
00:57:20.319 --> 00:57:29.517
<v COOPER>What . . . How was it different going to Mossville High School once it opened in '55, I think?

620
00:57:29.517 --> 00:57:40.338
<v SHELTON>That was the life then . . . because we had our own school and we didn't have a football field or anything,

621
00:57:40.338 --> 00:57:46.831
so everything that we played was on the outside . . . I mean games.

622
00:57:46.831 --> 00:57:54.947
Football . . . We did what they called soccer, but they called it kickball back there then.

623
00:57:54.947 --> 00:58:00.899
But we played the soccer ball out there in the back.

624
00:58:00.899 --> 00:58:10.097
Eventually, we did get a gym and all of the other games were held in the gym.

625
00:58:10.097 --> 00:58:12.261
The basketball games were.

626
00:58:12.261 --> 00:58:17.671
Now before that, we would have the outside basketball goals.

627
00:58:17.671 --> 00:58:20.918
<v COOPER>And did you have school dances?

628
00:58:20.918 --> 00:58:22.000
<v SHELTON>Huh?

629
00:58:22.000 --> 00:58:27.130
<v COOPER>Did you have any school dances or a prom at Mossville High?

630
00:58:27.130 --> 00:58:28.412
<v SHELTON>Yeah, we did.

631
00:58:28.412 --> 00:58:30.122
We had a prom.

632
00:58:30.122 --> 00:58:36.962
I don't know if I have . . . I may have a picture from my eighth grade prom.

633
00:58:36.962 --> 00:58:39.954
We did have an eighth grade prom . . .

634
00:58:39.954 --> 00:58:42.947
<v COOPER>Do you remember what that was like?

635
00:58:42.947 --> 00:58:45.084
<v SHELTON>. . . and a high school prom.

636
00:58:45.084 --> 00:58:45.511
Yeah.

637
00:58:45.511 --> 00:58:48.504
<v COOPER>Do you remember what that was like?

638
00:58:48.504 --> 00:58:50.641
Or where they held it?

639
00:58:50.641 --> 00:58:54.061
<v SHELTON>Let me see . . . What was that prom there?

640
00:58:54.061 --> 00:58:55.344
At the school.

641
00:58:55.344 --> 00:58:57.908
It was held at the school.

642
00:58:57.908 --> 00:59:09.023
<v COOPER>And how . . . So I know in your first interview you talked a little bit about having books that were handed down from schools with white students.

643
00:59:09.023 --> 00:59:12.015
How else did segregation affect your education?

644
00:59:12.015 --> 00:59:18.000
Or were you aware of it growing up in Mossville in elementary school?

645
00:59:18.000 --> 00:59:28.045
<v SHELTON>We knew that we were getting hand-me-down books now, but we just made the best of it.

646
00:59:28.045 --> 00:59:45.773
As a result, we had many kids to go on to be successful in school and become . . . what do they . . . citizens that were capable of helping the community and whatnot.

647
00:59:45.773 --> 00:59:47.545
We had lawyers.

648
00:59:47.545 --> 00:59:49.318
We had doctors.

649
00:59:49.318 --> 00:59:51.091
We had educators.

650
00:59:51.091 --> 00:59:58.182
We had other . . . We had entrepreneurs, all to come out of Mossville,

651
00:59:58.182 --> 01:00:07.636
even in spite of the fact that we had those hand-me-down tattered, I mean tattered books.

652
01:00:07.636 --> 01:00:16.500
But they were eager to see . . . the teachers were eager to see that we learned.

653
01:00:16.500 --> 01:00:21.227
Many of us were eager to do that.

654
01:00:21.227 --> 01:00:27.727
But most of us had parents that stayed on your time.

655
01:00:27.727 --> 01:00:37.182
They wanted to be . . . They wanted to see you prosper more than the way they did.

656
01:00:37.182 --> 01:00:41.909
So they encouraged you to do your best.

657
01:00:41.909 --> 01:00:47.818
They also saw to it that you did your homework.

658
01:00:47.818 --> 01:00:50.773
I know my parents did.

659
01:00:50.773 --> 01:00:56.091
They saw to it that you did your homework.

660
01:00:56.091 --> 01:01:15.000
Most of the others did because we would not have had as many to go to . . . further their education from that little town if they did not have parents that were concerned.

661
01:01:15.000 --> 01:01:28.000
Our parents didn't go to the school to raise hell; they went out there to help in any way that they could.

662
01:01:28.000 --> 01:01:42.182
I think that's why many of us . . . why we had so many successful students to come out of that little neck of the woods.

663
01:01:42.182 --> 01:01:48.682
<v COOPER>At what point did you know you were going to college?

664
01:01:48.682 --> 01:01:49.864
<v SHELTON>Oh, Lord.

665
01:01:49.864 --> 01:01:54.000
<v COOPER>What was that like for you?

666
01:01:54.000 --> 01:01:57.060
<v SHELTON>When I started at high school.

667
01:01:57.060 --> 01:02:02.160
My parents . . . My mother goes, "You are going to college.

668
01:02:02.160 --> 01:02:05.220
That's all there is to it.

669
01:02:05.220 --> 01:02:08.280
You're going to go to college."

670
01:02:08.280 --> 01:02:20.010
And at that time, we only had two that we could go to and that was either Grambling [State University] or Southern [University].

671
01:02:20.010 --> 01:02:26.130
Of course, later on after I started Southern, then McNeese was integrated.

672
01:02:26.130 --> 01:02:38.370
That was instilled in me from the beginning of high school that, no ifs and ands about it, you're going to go to school.

673
01:02:38.370 --> 01:02:41.430
You can forget doing anything else.

674
01:02:41.430 --> 01:02:45.000
You're going to go to school.

675
01:02:45.000 --> 01:02:49.163
<v COOPER>And was that the same for your classmates?

676
01:02:49.163 --> 01:02:49.684
<v SHELTON>Huh?

677
01:02:49.684 --> 01:02:53.847
<v COOPER>Did your classmates also go off to college?

678
01:02:53.847 --> 01:02:56.449
<v SHELTON>I had some to go.

679
01:02:56.449 --> 01:02:59.051
I don't recall how many.

680
01:02:59.051 --> 01:03:08.939
I had . . . I know one stayed here and went to college because she was able to go to McNeese.

681
01:03:08.939 --> 01:03:12.061
By that time McNeese was integrated.

682
01:03:12.061 --> 01:03:13.622
She went there.

683
01:03:13.622 --> 01:03:20.388
She might have been one of the first students to go to McNeese.

684
01:03:20.388 --> 01:03:21.949
I'm not sure.

685
01:03:21.949 --> 01:03:27.673
I didn't have any to go with me to Baton Rouge.

686
01:03:27.673 --> 01:03:29.755
I was by myself.

687
01:03:29.755 --> 01:03:36.000
<v COOPER>And what did you study when you went to Baton Rouge?

688
01:03:36.000 --> 01:03:37.346
<v SHELTON>Home economics.

689
01:03:37.346 --> 01:03:41.385
That's what I started off in.

690
01:03:41.385 --> 01:03:45.423
Ended up in elementary ed [education].

691
01:03:45.423 --> 01:03:49.462
Ended up in supervision in administration.

692
01:03:49.462 --> 01:03:58.212
So I went from home ec, to elementary ed., to supervision in administration.

693
01:03:58.212 --> 01:04:07.635
<v COOPER>Do you think that your schooling in Mossville influenced you to become an educator?

694
01:04:07.635 --> 01:04:08.308
<v SHELTON>Yes.

695
01:04:08.308 --> 01:04:11.000
I really do.

696
01:04:11.000 --> 01:04:22.695
<v COOPER>Do you have any special memories of a teacher or something in Mossville that stuck with you?

697
01:04:22.695 --> 01:04:23.383
<v SHELTON>Yes.

698
01:04:23.383 --> 01:04:38.518
One of my teachers, which she's still alive today, she was . . . When I started high school, she was the English teacher there.

699
01:04:38.518 --> 01:04:44.709
She was . . . She'd just gotten out of college herself.

700
01:04:44.709 --> 01:04:48.837
But she was not a pushover.

701
01:04:48.837 --> 01:04:55.028
She stayed on us and she was very articulate.

702
01:04:55.028 --> 01:04:59.844
I really used her as my mentor.

703
01:04:59.844 --> 01:05:10.163
Now, so far as down in the grades, when we had the two-room school house,

704
01:05:10.163 --> 01:05:19.794
Mrs. [Ezora Lyons?] and Mrs. P.M. [Prater] Washington were my idols at that time.

705
01:05:19.794 --> 01:05:25.298
And back there if you got into trouble,

706
01:05:25.298 --> 01:05:39.745
your parents best not know because you're going to get into trouble at home if you get in trouble at school.

707
01:05:39.745 --> 01:05:48.000
<v COOPER>What were some of your fondest memories growing up in Mossville?

708
01:05:48.000 --> 01:05:51.478
<v SHELTON>Staying out in the yard playing.

709
01:05:51.478 --> 01:06:03.652
Having the little minstrels come to our city once a year maybe, and it would be down on Old Spanish Trail.

710
01:06:03.652 --> 01:06:08.870
Traveling, like a traveling . . . I call them traveling minstrels.

711
01:06:08.870 --> 01:06:12.928
Well they had those back there then.

712
01:06:12.928 --> 01:06:18.145
<v COOPER>Could you tell us a little more about that?

713
01:06:18.145 --> 01:06:18.725
<v SHELTON>Huh?

714
01:06:18.725 --> 01:06:25.101
<v COOPER>Could you tell us a little more about the traveling minstrels?

715
01:06:25.101 --> 01:06:28.000
What was that like?

716
01:06:28.000 --> 01:06:39.746
<v SHELTON>Like the fairs, and they'd have them . . . might have two or three little Ferris wheels that you can get in.

717
01:06:39.746 --> 01:06:41.508
I can't recall.

718
01:06:41.508 --> 01:06:46.206
I just recall the fair with Ferris wheels.

719
01:06:46.206 --> 01:06:50.905
<v COOPER>Do you remember where that would take place?

720
01:06:50.905 --> 01:06:56.190
<v SHELTON>No, I can't recall when it would take place.

721
01:06:56.190 --> 01:07:01.476
I just remember the traveling minstrels coming to town.

722
01:07:01.476 --> 01:07:10.873
And another thing about school that I recall that was really one of my fondest memories,

723
01:07:10.873 --> 01:07:26.143
they always saw to it that we went on field trips because that's where I first got my . . . got a chance to go to the capitol.

724
01:07:26.143 --> 01:07:38.476
When we still had that two-room school house over there, we went . . . they took us on a trip to the capitol.

725
01:07:38.476 --> 01:07:43.762
We went on . . . We've gone to New Orleans [Louisiana].

726
01:07:43.762 --> 01:07:56.095
See because many of us probably wouldn't have never even left out of Mossville to even go across to Lake Charles.

727
01:07:56.095 --> 01:08:04.905
But anyway, we took . . . We went to New Orleans to the Cabildo, St. Louis Cathedral.

728
01:08:04.905 --> 01:08:09.016
We went to visit the Tabasco factory.

729
01:08:09.016 --> 01:08:11.952
That's down around St. Martinville.

730
01:08:11.952 --> 01:08:17.238
And they had the bird sanctuary, we went there.

731
01:08:17.238 --> 01:08:31.921
They tried to see to it that we had a wide variety of experiences as soon . . . as much as we could get at that time.

732
01:08:31.921 --> 01:08:43.079
In other words, they introduced us to some of the cultural events that were taking place there in Louisiana,

733
01:08:43.079 --> 01:08:47.778
or cultural scenes that were in our state.

734
01:08:47.778 --> 01:08:56.000
That was really one of my fondest memories . . . going on those field trips.

735
01:08:56.000 --> 01:09:06.577
<v COOPER>Do you remember who the community leaders were when you were growing up in Mossville?

736
01:09:06.577 --> 01:09:13.628
<v SHELTON>[agrees] Mr. Josh Rigmaiden was one of our community leaders.

737
01:09:13.628 --> 01:09:17.859
<v COOPER>What do you remember about him?

738
01:09:17.859 --> 01:09:20.679
<v SHELTON>He had a store.

739
01:09:20.679 --> 01:09:40.423
And when political leaders would come to town they'd always go to Josh and Josh would always have the big barbeques and what not for the political candidates.

740
01:09:40.423 --> 01:09:50.295
That's who I remember as being one of the leaders for sure was Josh.

741
01:09:50.295 --> 01:09:56.641
<v COOPER>Do you remember if he had any involvement in . . .

742
01:09:56.641 --> 01:09:58.051
<v SHELTON>Excuse me.

743
01:09:58.051 --> 01:10:11.449
<v COOPER>. . . in getting the Mossville school built, the high school built, or if he helped with any specific political issue?

744
01:10:11.449 --> 01:10:20.615
<v SHELTON>I'm sure he did, but . . . I'm sure he did, but I can't recall.

745
01:10:20.615 --> 01:10:24.141
But I'm sure he did.

746
01:10:24.141 --> 01:10:28.372
Josh along with possibly McKeever Edwards.

747
01:10:28.372 --> 01:10:34.718
I don't know . . . maybe one of the Lemelle’s, perhaps.

748
01:10:34.718 --> 01:10:46.000
But I know those, Josh and probably McKeever Edwards for sure, because he was another.

749
01:10:46.000 --> 01:10:51.031
<v COOPER>What does Mossville mean to you?

750
01:10:51.031 --> 01:10:57.740
<v SHELTON>What does Mossville mean . . . It means so much.

751
01:10:57.740 --> 01:11:04.448
Mossville means . . . I'm trying to put it together.

752
01:11:04.448 --> 01:11:13.672
My roots . . . I'm trying . . . That's where it all began for me.

753
01:11:13.672 --> 01:11:17.865
My roots, I would say.

754
01:11:17.865 --> 01:11:22.057
That's where it all began.

755
01:11:22.057 --> 01:11:27.927
From your roots, everything else sprouts out.

756
01:11:27.927 --> 01:11:32.958
So Mossville is considered my roots.

757
01:11:32.958 --> 01:11:40.505
So that's where it all began, with my roots.

758
01:11:40.505 --> 01:11:57.276
I would consider Mossville my roots, because I wouldn't be able to . . . If it had not been for my roots,

759
01:11:57.276 --> 01:12:07.339
which would be Mossville, I don't know what else . . . where I'd be.

760
01:12:07.339 --> 01:12:14.047
So Mossville, that's what it means to me.

761
01:12:14.047 --> 01:12:15.724
My roots.

762
01:12:15.724 --> 01:12:22.432
Let me see what I put in here.

763
01:12:22.432 --> 01:12:40.042
I don't know if I put anything in here about . . . I know I said there was always excitement in our community.

764
01:12:40.042 --> 01:12:45.073
Well, that's what I would think.

765
01:12:45.073 --> 01:12:51.781
Mossville . . . those would be my . . . that's my foundation.

766
01:12:51.781 --> 01:12:56.812
Mossville is my foundation, my roots.

767
01:12:56.812 --> 01:13:01.844
That's what it means to me.

768
01:13:01.844 --> 01:13:18.615
<v COOPER>Any what year, or when, was the family home . . . I know you mentioned that it was demolished on Prater Road.

769
01:13:18.615 --> 01:13:21.969
When did that happen?

770
01:13:21.969 --> 01:13:23.646
<v SHELTON>Last year.

771
01:13:23.646 --> 01:13:25.323
<v COOPER>Last year.

772
01:13:25.323 --> 01:13:27.000
<v SHELTON>[Agrees]

773
01:13:27.000 --> 01:13:32.456
<v COOPER>What will you miss the most about Mossville?

774
01:13:32.456 --> 01:13:34.503
<v SHELTON>My family home.

775
01:13:34.503 --> 01:13:36.549
My family home.

776
01:13:36.549 --> 01:13:42.687
And a lot of my friends that were there.

777
01:13:42.687 --> 01:13:43.369
Yeah.

778
01:13:43.369 --> 01:13:48.144
Let's see . . . Dorothy lived right behind us.

779
01:13:48.144 --> 01:13:52.918
I'll miss our family . . . our little neighborhood.

780
01:13:52.918 --> 01:13:59.056
<v COOPER>Well, it's been about an hour and fifteen minutes.

781
01:13:59.056 --> 01:14:05.195
So we'll probably . . . Time kind of flew by, huh?

782
01:14:05.195 --> 01:14:07.923
We'll probably wrap up.

783
01:14:07.923 --> 01:14:14.062
But is there anything else you want to share?

784
01:14:14.062 --> 01:14:22.246
Is there any other memory of Mossville that you want to share?

785
01:14:22.246 --> 01:14:27.703
<v SHELTON>No. Let's see what I have in here.

786
01:14:27.703 --> 01:14:35.887
Did I tell you how we used to go to the beaches?

787
01:14:35.887 --> 01:14:38.615
We’d have hay rides.

788
01:14:38.615 --> 01:14:39.979
<v COOPER>Hay rides?

789
01:14:39.979 --> 01:14:40.662
<v SHELTON>Yeah.

790
01:14:40.662 --> 01:14:46.118
You don't know nothing about hay rides either.

791
01:14:46.118 --> 01:14:59.759
You have this big truck and have hay on the back of the truck . . . on the bed of the truck.

792
01:14:59.759 --> 01:15:06.579
And all these kids would be on the truck riding.

793
01:15:06.579 --> 01:15:08.626
That was fun.

794
01:15:08.626 --> 01:15:14.082
We did it to the beaches in Cameron.

795
01:15:14.082 --> 01:15:22.267
That was big time when we'd go to the beaches in Cameron.

796
01:15:22.267 --> 01:15:37.954
I was saying . . . Oh, okay I have here that the first female to head a university system taught math at Mossville High School.

797
01:15:37.954 --> 01:15:40.000
<v COOPER>Oh, wow.

798
01:15:40.000 --> 01:15:46.538
<v SHELTON>The first female to ever head a university system.

799
01:15:46.538 --> 01:15:55.981
A former McNeese State University professor, Dr. Whitney Harris, was a Mossville graduate.

800
01:15:55.981 --> 01:16:03.972
I don't know if y'all have heard of Dr. Whitney Harris.

801
01:16:03.972 --> 01:16:09.057
He taught at McNeese for a while.

802
01:16:09.057 --> 01:16:14.868
Professional football player, Richard Lee, was from Mossville.

803
01:16:14.868 --> 01:16:25.764
Johnny Comeaux was an artist, he was from Mossville and also a professional basketball player.

804
01:16:25.764 --> 01:16:26.491
<v COOPER>Wow.

805
01:16:26.491 --> 01:16:38.113
<v SHELTON>And he had a . . . And my husband was the first black TV news man for KPLC.

806
01:16:38.113 --> 01:16:38.840
<v COOPER>Wow.

807
01:16:38.840 --> 01:16:41.019
He's from Westlake?

808
01:16:41.019 --> 01:16:43.925
<v SHELTON>He graduated from Mossville.

809
01:16:43.925 --> 01:16:45.377
<v COOPER>Oh, wow.

810
01:16:45.377 --> 01:16:46.830
<v SHELTON>[laughs] Yes.

811
01:16:46.830 --> 01:16:51.915
<v COOPER>Is that where you met, in Mossville?

812
01:16:51.915 --> 01:16:55.547
<v SHELTON>Mossville, at the high school.

813
01:16:55.547 --> 01:16:57.000
Yeah.

814
01:16:57.000 --> 01:16:59.961
<v COOPER>How did you meet?

815
01:16:59.961 --> 01:17:09.583
<v SHELTON>Well, when did I . . . Oh, we had a . . . At the swimming pool there.

816
01:17:09.583 --> 01:17:13.283
We had a swimming pool.

817
01:17:13.283 --> 01:17:19.205
<v COOPER>There was a swimming pool at Mossville High?

818
01:17:19.205 --> 01:17:23.646
<v SHELTON>No. Not very far from Mossville.

819
01:17:23.646 --> 01:17:29.567
But we met each other in high school.

820
01:17:29.567 --> 01:17:33.268
We were in high school.

821
01:17:33.268 --> 01:17:42.150
But he really spoke to me and such at that swimming pool.

822
01:17:42.150 --> 01:17:53.252
I'm looking at all the folks that have come from out of that little town.

823
01:17:53.252 --> 01:18:09.535
Bankers, teachers, counselors, nurses, hospital administrators, entrepreneurs, accountants, engineers, plant operators, spiritual leaders, vocalists, law enforcement agents, military officers and community activists.

824
01:18:09.535 --> 01:18:13.236
Dorothy was a community activist.

825
01:18:13.236 --> 01:18:17.677
Have you heard of Mickey Smith?

826
01:18:17.677 --> 01:18:20.638
<v COOPER>That name sounds familiar.

827
01:18:20.638 --> 01:18:26.559
<v SHELTON>He's a music educator at Maplewood High School.

828
01:18:26.559 --> 01:18:29.520
A Grammy Award winner.

829
01:18:29.520 --> 01:18:31.000
<v COOPER>Wow.

830
01:18:31.000 --> 01:18:32.272
<v SHELTON>He's from Mossville.

831
01:18:32.272 --> 01:18:35.664
That's why I want you to have this.

832
01:18:35.664 --> 01:18:39.480
I don't know if it will help you any.

833
01:18:39.480 --> 01:18:40.752
<v COOPER>Yeah, that's wonderful.

834
01:18:40.752 --> 01:18:47.112
So, just for the record, you're reading off of a document that you typed up.

835
01:18:47.112 --> 01:18:47.536
<v SHELTON>Yeah.

836
01:18:47.536 --> 01:18:49.232
<v COOPER>And what's it called?

837
01:18:49.232 --> 01:18:53.048
<v SHELTON>“Mossville through Our Eyes: A Walk Down Memory Lane.”

838
01:18:53.048 --> 01:18:55.168
<v COOPER>“A Walk Down Memory Lane.”

839
01:18:55.168 --> 01:18:56.016
Very cool.

840
01:18:56.016 --> 01:19:00.680
Alright, well I think we're going to stop here for now.

841
01:19:00.680 --> 01:19:05.344
So on behalf of the museum, the Calcasieu Museum, and LSU,

842
01:19:05.344 --> 01:19:12.552
thank you so much for allowing us to come into your home and to interview you again.

843
01:19:12.552 --> 01:19:14.248
Yeah, we're very thankful.

844
01:19:14.248 --> 01:19:18.488
So thank you for sharing all your stories with us.

845
01:19:18.488 --> 01:19:22.304
And with that, I'd like to close the interview.

846
01:19:22.304 --> 01:19:24.000
You could stop.

